Add a platformer: Delve (n-gon-inspired cave roguelite)
Context
The site has four self-contained canvas games. The user wants a fifth: a platformer modelled loosely on n-gon. It should share n-gon's themes without copying them: large levels, healing, lots of enemies that visibly die, mouse-aimed shooting, and powerups. The powerups must not be n-gon's (heal / ammo / research / tech / field / gun). On top of that, the game should be:

a little roguelike: runs, upgrades that last for the run, and rerolled layouts;
cave-dweller themed;
built around a guided, large and tall tunnel mechanic: big winding cave systems that lead you along one route.
The levels are hand-made, but some marked spots reroll every run. For example, a few designated blocks may rarely become lava, and a jump pad might become a staircase. The randomness must never make a level impossible.

The CLAUDE.md constraints apply:

plain HTML/CSS/JS, with no build step and no dependencies (no matter.js, unlike n-gon);
no code shared with the other games;
LF line endings;
logic changes go in through a PR.
The game
Delve is a descent through 5 cave depths in one run.

The run (light roguelike)
A run is Depths 1 → 5, ending with the Warden at the bottom.
You have one life: dying (HP 0, or falling into a bottomless pit) ends the run, and the next run starts again at Depth 1 with fresh rolls and no relics.
Spikes and lava deal heavy damage rather than killing instantly, so a single misstep is costly but survivable. Only bottomless pits kill outright.
Between depths you pick 1 of 3 relics. These are permanent for the run, for example:
+max HP
+shot damage
bigger light radius
sparks heal more
longer timed-powerup duration
a chance for kills to drop crystals
Every run rerolls the variant spots (see below), so no two runs play quite the same.
Saved in localStorage (try/catch): deepest depth reached, best full-run time, and total kills.
Guided tunnels (the core level shape)
Each depth is a large hand-made cave of roughly 200–300 × 60–90 tiles. It is one main tunnel that winds horizontally and drops down tall vertical shafts, with the camera scrolling both ways.
The way is guided rather than signposted. Glow-moss trails along the main route light up as you approach, so you always know which way leads deeper. Side pockets off the tunnel hold crystals, relic-adjacent loot and tougher enemy nests.
Each depth starts at the top-left area and ends at a descent hole lower down. Dropping in finishes the depth.
The tall shafts are traversed with ledges, one-way planks, jump pads/staircases and moving lifts. Some shafts go up, so the route isn't just "fall".
Cave look
The world is dark outside the player's lantern radius.
Light also comes from lava, crystals, glow-moss, beacons and muzzle flashes. This is done with an offscreen darkness mask and radial cut-outs.
The world is squares; living things are not.
Everything static is drawn on the square tile grid: rock, lava, planks, moss, crystals at rest and beacons.
The background is squares too: a dimmer, parallax-scrolled layer of square back-wall tiles. There are no curved silhouettes or smooth stalactites.
Squares can vary in shade and carry square-pixel detail, but the blocky grid stays obvious.
Living and moving things ignore the grid. The player, enemies, bullets, sparks, drops and particles move freely in continuous coordinates. They can be any shape: circles, polygons, or a body with legs or wings. Moving lifts slide smoothly but are still drawn as rows of squares.
Rock is in browns and slates. The player and shots are cyan, enemies are warm red/amber, and drips and dust particles fill the air.
This is deliberately unlike n-gon's white line-art.
Controls
WASD/arrows to move.
W/Space to jump: holding jumps higher, with coyote time and jump buffering.
Mouse to aim; click or hold to fire.
Shift to dash (while Blink has charges).
Esc to pause.
Health and healing (n-gon theme, not its heal powerup)
You have an HP bar, with brief invulnerability after a hit.
Life sparks sometimes drop from kills and drift toward you.
Lighting a beacon heals you to full. Beacons are a one-time heal; there are no respawn points.
Enemies
Each enemy has HP, flashes when hit, and on death bursts into fragments plus a light flash, sometimes dropping a spark:

Crawler: patrols ledges.
Bat: a flyer that swoops once it sees you.
Spitter: wall- or ceiling-mounted, lobs arcing acid.
Brute: tanky, charges when you're level with it.
Grub nest: spawns crawlers until destroyed.
Warden: a multi-phase boss at Depth 5.
Powerups (all new, not n-gon's)
These are crystals found in levels or rare drops. "Permanent" means for the rest of the run. Picking up a permanent one you already have heals you instead.

Splitter (permanent): shots fork into 3.
Ricochet (permanent): shots bounce off rock twice.
Overclock (20s): fire rate ×2.
Featherfall (45s): a double jump and a glide.
Aegis (until used): absorbs the next 2 hits.
Blink (3 charges): each Shift press dashes through enemies and uses a charge. The pickup ends when all 3 are spent.
Flare (10s): a huge light radius that reveals side pockets.
Because Splitter and Ricochet are permanent, they are rarer: at most one fixed spot per depth, plus a low drop chance.

Per-run variant spots
These markers in the level strings are rolled with a seeded RNG at run start:

L: a block that becomes lava at about 8%. It is only placed where it isn't the sole foothold.
J: a jump pad or a staircase. Both reach the same ledge.
?: an enemy slot that may roll empty or roll one of the allowed types.
*: a crystal slot that may roll a random powerup or nothing.
A scratchpad reachability check runs over every depth with every variant at its worst case, so each depth is guaranteed completable.

Files
New folder delve/, which shares nothing with the other games:

index.html: the canvas (960×544, scaled to fit), a HUD (HP, depth, active powerups with timers or charges, relics, kills, time), overlays (title/run start, relic pick, pause, death, run over, victory), and the ← All games back link in the same corner as tank-duel/index.html.
style.css: the cave theme.
levels.js: the depth row-strings plus a legend comment. They are generated by a scratchpad script that keeps rows aligned. The existing small draft generator gets replaced by a tunnel-oriented one.
game.js: input, fixed-timestep physics, tile collision (x then y, one-way planks), variant rolling, the run and relic state, the player, bullets, enemy AI per type, powerups, drops, lighting, particles, camera, rendering and saving.
README.md: in the shape of tank-duel/README.md.
thumbnail.webp: a real in-game capture taken with headless Chromium through canvas.toDataURL('image/webp').
